// =============================================================================
// PostFxBloomProgramsPC.cpp  (pc/gcm/renderengine)
//
// [PC platform leaf] GENERATED FILE -- do not hand-edit. Regenerate with the
// recipe recorded below. Sibling of PostFxProgramsPC.cpp, which carries the
// post-fx COMPOSITE pair; this file carries the six BLOOM programs.
//
// BrnPostFxBloom draws three passes through three vertex/pixel pairs
// (BrnPostFxBloom::Construct @0x82401740 builds all six):
//     down-sample / bright pass  PrepareDownSampleBuffer         @0x82401AE8
//     "new" one-pass blur        Generate1PassBlurredBloomBuffer @0x82401F50
//     "old" separable 5-tap      Generate2PassBlurredBloomBuffer @0x82402308
// Construct clears mbUseNewBloom, so the SHIPPING path is down-sample + the
// "old" two-pass blur; the "new" blur pair is reconstructed all the same
// (leaving one slot empty would re-arm the console's own NULL assert).
//
// AUTHORED, NOT CONVERTED. Like the composite and the sky dome, the bloom
// programs are NOT in SHADERS.BNDL: they are Xenos microcode embedded in the
// guest image and handed straight to the program-buffer registrar --
//     gacBloomDSVertexProgram       0x8203E6F8  348 B
//     gacBloomDSPixelProgram        0x8203E858  516 B
//     gacBloomBlurVertexProgram     0x8203EA60  368 B
//     gacBloomBlurPixelProgram      0x8203EBD0  420 B
//     gacBloomBlurOldVertexProgram  0x8203ED78  404 B
//     gacBloomBlurOldPixelProgram   0x8203EF10  424 B
// (all six are byte-exact string literals at the top of
// GameSource/Graphics/PostFx/BrnPostFxBloom.cpp). Xenos microcode cannot run on
// D3D9, so each was disassembled instruction by instruction with
// tools/assets/shaders/xenos.py and re-expressed as HLSL in
// tools/assets/shaders/brn_postfx_bloom.fx. The constant surface is not
// guessed -- it is the CTAB interned in each microcode package:
//     DS vertex      : c0 kUvOffset_00_01, c1 kUvOffset_02_03
//     DS pixel       : c0 kDotWithWhiteLevel (float3),
//                      c1 kThresholdAndScale (float3), s0 SamplerSource
//     blur vertex    : c0 kUvOffset_00_01, c1 kUvOffset_02_03
//     blur pixel     : s0 SamplerSource  (NO float constants -- its 0.25 tap
//                      scale is a literal in the microcode's own constant block)
//     blurOld vertex : c0 kUvOffset_00_01, c1 kUvOffset_02_03,
//                      c2 kUvOffset_04_05
//     blurOld pixel  : c0 kTapWeights0_3, c1 kTapWeights4, s0 SamplerSource
// and that set is EXACTLY the set BrnPostFxBloom::Construct already asks for by
// name through renderengine::ProgramBuffer::GetVariableHandleByName -- eleven
// handles, eleven CTAB entries, no name on either side without a partner. The
// PC build reproduces the register assignment register for register (the .fx
// pins each one with register(); fxc's own report confirms it). The engine
// still resolves by NAME, so nothing depends on the numbering.
//
// WHAT THE THREE PAIRS COMPUTE (recovered from the microcode; the per-slot
// mapping is in the wave report and in the .fx's own comments):
//
//   DOWN-SAMPLE (bright pass). The vertex program passes the clip-space quad
//   through untransformed and emits FOUR source UVs as two float4s:
//       TEXCOORD0 = uv.xyxy + kUvOffset_00_01     // taps 0,1
//       TEXCOORD1 = uv.xyxy + kUvOffset_02_03     // taps 2,3
//   (PrepareDownSampleBuffer fills those with the 2x2 box (-du,-dv) (+du,-dv)
//   (-du,+dv) (+du,+dv)). The pixel program bright-passes each tap and sums:
//       tap_i    = tex2D(SamplerSource, uv_i).rgb
//       bright_i = saturate((dot(tap_i, kDotWithWhiteLevel)
//                            - kThresholdAndScale.x) * tap_i)
//       oC0.rgb  = (bright_0 + bright_1 + bright_2 + bright_3)
//                  * kThresholdAndScale.y
//   -- i.e. the SUBTRACT-THEN-RESCALE the two pixel constants describe, with the
//   four-tap average and the 1/whiteLevel folded into kDotWithWhiteLevel.
//
//   "NEW" ONE-PASS BLUR. Same four UVs; the pixel program is a plain box:
//       oC0.rgb = (t0 + t1 + t2 + t3) * 0.25
//   The vertex program also exports the RAW uv as TEXCOORD2, which its pixel
//   program never reads -- dead on the console too, reproduced as encoded.
//
//   "OLD" SEPARABLE FIVE-TAP GAUSSIAN (the shipping blur). Three offset
//   constants give six UV slots, five used:
//       oC0.rgb = t0*kTapWeights0_3.x + t1*kTapWeights0_3.y
//               + t2*kTapWeights0_3.z + t3*kTapWeights0_3.w
//               + t4*kTapWeights4.x
//   Generate2PassBlurredBloomBuffer runs it twice, offsets in u then in v.
//
// ALPHA IS NOT RECOVERED -- IT IS INDETERMINATE ON THE CONSOLE. All three
// console pixel programs finish with the scalar unit's `retain_prev
// export0.___w [= PS]` and none of the three ever issues a scalar op, so the
// exported alpha is the previous-scalar residual. It is never read back: every
// consumer of the bloom / work targets fetches rgb only (`tfetch r?.xyz_` in
// all three blur programs and in the composite's SamplerBloom fetch). The PC
// programs write 0 so the result is deterministic. Flagged, not pretended.
//
// VERTEX DECLARATION. All three pairs share the descriptor
// BrnPostFxBloom::Construct builds -- element 0 format 0x2A23B9 (FLOAT3),
// elementType 1 -> POSITION0; element 1 format 0x2C23A5 (FLOAT2), elementType
// 6 -> TEXCOORD0 (the kauVertexFormatDefaults table, ImmediateModePCLeaf.cpp).
// That is byte-identical to the Parameters block BrnPostFxShader::Construct
// builds for the composite, whose PC pair draws through it today; the vertex
// programs below declare exactly `float3 POSITION` + `float2 TEXCOORD0` and
// pass POSITION through with w = 1, which is what the console does
// (`tfetch r1.xyz1` + `max export62, r1, r1`).
//
// Build recipe (the same flags convert_shaders_bundle.py::compile_entry and
// PostFxProgramsPC.cpp use; /Zpr (row-major packing) is carried so the recipe
// stays identical to every other converted PC program -- none of these six has
// a matrix constant, so it changes nothing here):
//     fxc /nologo /T vs_3_0 /E mainDSVS      /O2 /Zpr /Fo bloom_ds_vs.fxo      brn_postfx_bloom.fx
//     fxc /nologo /T ps_3_0 /E mainDSPS      /O2 /Zpr /Fo bloom_ds_ps.fxo      brn_postfx_bloom.fx
//     fxc /nologo /T vs_3_0 /E mainBlurVS    /O2 /Zpr /Fo bloom_blur_vs.fxo    brn_postfx_bloom.fx
//     fxc /nologo /T ps_3_0 /E mainBlurPS    /O2 /Zpr /Fo bloom_blur_ps.fxo    brn_postfx_bloom.fx
//     fxc /nologo /T vs_3_0 /E mainBlurOldVS /O2 /Zpr /Fo bloom_blurold_vs.fxo brn_postfx_bloom.fx
//     fxc /nologo /T ps_3_0 /E mainBlurOldPS /O2 /Zpr /Fo bloom_blurold_ps.fxo brn_postfx_bloom.fx
// then wrapped by tools/assets/shaders/shader_transcode.py ::
// build_pc_program_buffer(bytecode, shader_type) into platform-4
// ShaderProgramBuffer primary images (0x14-byte ProgramBufferData header +
// D3D9 bytecode at +0x14 + 8-byte ProgramVariableDescriptor table rebuilt from
// the bytecode's CTAB + interned NUL name strings). Only the primary is
// carried: muMicrocodePart3 is 0, there is no Xenos physical block. Adopt them
// with renderengine::ProgramBufferPC_Adopt (programbuffer.h:123), which copies
// the image into low-4 GB memory and rebases the descriptor name offsets so
// GetVariableHandleByName keeps working unchanged.
//
// Provenance:
//     fxc.exe   C:\Program Files (x86)\Windows Kits\10\bin\10.0.26100.0\x64
//               (Microsoft (R) Direct3D Shader Compiler 10.1, file version
//               10.0.26100.4654)
//     raw D3D9 bytecode   316 / 632 / 340 / 344 / 380 / 488 bytes
